<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    INTERNET is declared, and declaring it is the point of this file.

    The native head shipped with no INTERNET permission at all for months and
    nobody noticed, because every model had been sideloaded over a cable. A voice
    is 60-150 MB and is downloaded on request, so without this the first "Hear it"
    fails in a way that looks like a broken server.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
	<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
	<!--
	    RECORD_AUDIO must be in the manifest or requestPermissions is a SILENT
	    no-op and AudioRecord yields silence - which looks exactly like a wake
	    word that does not work rather than like a permission that was never
	    asked for.
	-->
	<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO" />
	<!--
	    MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS is required by every audio EFFECT this app attaches to its capture - AutomaticGainControl, NoiseSuppressor and AcousticEchoCanceler, see AndroidAudio.cs.
	    Without it the audio server refuses the request ("Request requires android.permission.MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS"), the effects fail to configure ("SET_CONFIG returned Invalid argument"), and on this Huawei the capture restarts every ~20s with "record is not occpied, check mic mute" - so the wake word listens to nothing.
	    Measured on the P30.
	-->
	<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.MODIFY_AUDIO_SETTINGS" />
	<!--
	    REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES, because Circle AI is two apps and this is the one with the icon.
	    The other half is the brain - no icon, large, and the only thing that can answer a question - and a phone with nothing on it cannot reach a store or the mesh to get it. The route that needs nothing is somebody who already has Circle AI handing over the installer through Android's share sheet, and receiving it means this app asks Android to install a package.
	    NOT A RUNTIME PERMISSION AND THERE IS NO DIALOG. From Android 8 it is a per-app switch on a Settings screen somebody has to visit - declaring it here only makes that switch exist. ServicePairing.InstallPermissionSettings takes them straight to this app's own, rather than a list of every app on the phone.
	    Declared because nothing fails at build time without it: a missing permission in a split app is silent until somebody is standing in front of the broken screen.
	-->
	<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES" />
	<!--
	    SEVEN PERMISSIONS LEFT WITH THE RESIDENT LISTENER AND THE DECLARATIONS DID NOT.

	    This head used to run the foreground service, hold the wake lock, post the
	    microphone notification and come back after a reboot. All of that is
	    CircleAIService's now - this app has three files under Platforms/Android
	    (this manifest, MainActivity, MainApplication), no Service, no
	    BroadcastReceiver, and references neither CircleAI.Device nor
	    CircleAI.Assistant.Device. Nothing in this APK can use any of them.

	    So these are gone:

	      FOREGROUND_SERVICE               no Service in this package
	      FOREGROUND_SERVICE_DATA_SYNC     ditto
	      FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MICROPHONE    ditto - and this is the one that reads
	                                       worst on a store listing: a thin client
	                                       asking to hold a microphone in the
	                                       background for work another app does
	      POST_NOTIFICATIONS               ServiceAnnouncer posts nothing, on purpose
	                                       - "the shade belongs to the service that
	                                       is actually doing the work"
	      WAKE_LOCK                        no PowerManager call survives here
	      RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED           BootReceiver went with the models
	      REQUEST_IGNORE_BATTERY_OPTIMIZATIONS
	                                       only grants the per-package dialog, and
	                                       the package needing the exemption is the
	                                       SERVICE. An app cannot ask for another
	                                       app's. See ServiceSetup.AllowBackgroundAsync,
	                                       which opens the settings list instead -
	                                       that intent needs no permission at all.

	    A permission a build cannot exercise is not harmless. It is the first thing
	    a reviewer reads and the only thing most people will ever see of this split.
	-->
	<!--
	    hasFragileUserData: on Android 10+ the uninstall dialog OFFERS to keep this
	    app's data. One attribute, and a reinstall stops wiping 250 MB of models
	    somebody already paid for - which is the mechanism Android provides for
	    data an app NEEDS and cannot cheaply refetch. No storage permission is
	    asked for: the models are app data and stay in private storage.
	-->
	<!--
	    android:icon HAS TO BE HERE, and its absence is invisible in every build
	    log. MauiIcon generates every density - res/mipmap-*/appicon*.png and the
	    adaptive appicon.xml were all sitting in the APK - but declaring
	    <application> in this file with explicit attributes means NOTHING pointed
	    at them: `aapt2 dump badging` read back `icon=''`, so Android had no icon
	    to draw and fell back to its own generic tile. The launcher showed a white
	    jigsaw piece on a blue square, sharp-cornered next to every other app's
	    rounded icon, for as long as this line was missing.

	    The label is not the same story - that one MAUI injects from
	    ApplicationTitle, which is why the name read correctly all along and made
	    the icon look like a rendering quirk rather than a missing attribute.
	-->
	<!--
	    WHO ELSE ON THIS PHONE CAN PLAY MUSIC.

	    From Android 11 an app cannot see other apps unless it declares what it
	    is looking for. Without this, ResolveActivity on the media search intent
	    returns null even when Spotify, VLC and the phone's own player are all
	    installed - so "play Coldplay" would answer "there is no music app on
	    this phone", which is both wrong and unfalsifiable from the outside.

	    An INTENT filter rather than a package name: naming apps would make the
	    feature depend on which ones somebody happens to use, and this asks the
	    only question that matters - who can take this request.
	-->
	<queries>
		<intent>
			<action android:name="android.media.action.MEDIA_PLAY_FROM_SEARCH" />
		</intent>
		<!--
		    THE CIRCLEAI SERVICE, BY PACKAGE. Same Android 11 visibility rule as the
		    media query above, and the failure is nastier because it is silent:
		    without this, PackageManager.GetPackageInfo throws NameNotFound on a
		    device where the service is installed and running, so
		    CircleAiLinkClient.IsInstalled returns false, no bind is ever attempted,
		    and nothing logs an error. The app simply behaves as though CircleAI
		    were not on the phone.

		    By PACKAGE rather than by intent, deliberately: there is exactly one
		    host and its identity is the thing being checked. An intent query would
		    let any app claiming the action answer, which for a component that
		    holds the person's memory is the wrong shape of question.
		-->
		<package android:name="com.bhengubv.circleai.service" />
	</queries>

	<application android:allowBackup="true"
	             android:supportsRtl="true"
	             android:icon="@mipmap/appicon"
	             android:roundIcon="@mipmap/appicon_round"
	             android:hasFragileUserData="true"></application>
</manifest>
